### Tone: End-of-game award: Round Numbers, the most answers with one significant digit (like 5 000 or 2 million).
### $count: those answers. Amused at the tidiness; precision was not invited.

award-round-numbers-1 =
    The Round Numbers award goes to { $name }: { $count ->
        [one] one perfectly round answer.
       *[other] { $count } perfectly round answers.
    } Tidy. Clean. Almost certainly approximate.
award-round-numbers-2 = { $name }, you only deal in round numbers. Reality comes in decimals. You’ve chosen not to acknowledge that.
award-round-numbers-3 = Round Numbers: { $name }. Every answer looked like a price tag. A suspiciously neat price tag.
award-round-numbers-4 = { $name } estimates in nice, round, satisfying numbers. The truth is lumpier. The truth is always lumpier.
award-round-numbers-5 = The award for tidy estimating goes to { $name }. Fermi would approve. Your score, less so.
award-round-numbers-6 = { $name }, a number with one digit and a lot of zeros, every time. You’re not estimating. You’re rounding with confidence.
award-round-numbers-7 = Round Numbers: { $name }. Precision is overrated, apparently. Bold stance.
award-round-numbers-8 = { $name } never met a digit they couldn’t round off. I admire the commitment to zeros.
award-round-numbers-9 = The Round Numbers award goes to { $name }, whose answers could all be written on a birthday cake.
award-round-numbers-10 = { $name }: always a round number. If the answer was ever exactly round, you’d have been unbeatable. It never was.
